1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,553
Since the 1950s, Louisiana
has lost enough wetlands to cover

2
00:00:03,611 --> 00:00:05,108
the entire state of Rhode Island.

3
00:00:05,132 --> 00:00:07,686
But in one corner of its coast,
the land is growing.

4
00:00:07,721 --> 00:00:11,598
This is the Mississippi River Delta,
the seventh largest delta on Earth.

5
00:00:11,668 --> 00:00:15,348
It was built the slow way,
centimeter by centimeter, from mud,

6
00:00:15,476 --> 00:00:18,146
sand and plant material
carried down by the river.

7
00:00:18,587 --> 00:00:22,662
Landsat satellites have watched this coast
since the early nineteen seventies.

8
00:00:22,871 --> 00:00:26,133
Using that record from 1984 to 2020,

9
00:00:26,249 --> 00:00:30,115
NASA-funded researchers measured how
much marsh turned into open water.

10
00:00:30,208 --> 00:00:33,378
The answer: nearly twenty-one
square miles every year.

11
00:00:33,587 --> 00:00:35,758
That's about fifty-four square kilometers.

12
00:00:35,851 --> 00:00:37,650
Some of it drowned as the sea rose.

13
00:00:37,720 --> 00:00:41,597
Some was torn up by hurricanes,
or cut by oil and gas infrastructure.

14
00:00:41,690 --> 00:00:43,362
But the biggest driver was engineering.

15
00:00:43,443 --> 00:00:47,542
Levees and thousands of miles of canals
cut many wetlands off from the river

16
00:00:47,623 --> 00:00:49,097
and the sediment it carries.

17
00:00:49,190 --> 00:00:52,348
The Terrebonne and Barataria
basins failed to build new soil.

18
00:00:52,383 --> 00:00:55,123
Together, they lost more than one
hundred eighty square miles.

19
00:00:55,215 --> 00:00:57,282
Now look west, at the Atchafalaya Basin.

20
00:00:57,398 --> 00:00:59,825
Here the river still spreads
its mud across the marsh.

21
00:00:59,941 --> 00:01:03,261
Over the same years, it gained
thirty-three square miles of new land.

22
00:01:03,470 --> 00:01:07,719
And at the mouth of the Mississippi,
the Bird's Foot Delta gained forty-three more.

23
00:01:07,963 --> 00:01:11,411
So why does one basin grow
while its neighbor disappears?

24
00:01:11,736 --> 00:01:15,220
To find out, NASA sent
a mission called Delta-X.

25
00:01:15,603 --> 00:01:17,159
Teams went in by boat,

26
00:01:17,217 --> 00:01:20,584
measuring how much water and sediment
flows through the channels.

27
00:01:20,967 --> 00:01:25,077
Others waded into the marsh to sample
plants and the soil they help build.

28
00:01:25,402 --> 00:01:27,805
Above them, three planes
flew at the same time.

29
00:01:28,188 --> 00:01:31,718
One carried AirSWOT, measuring
the height and slope of the water.

30
00:01:31,787 --> 00:01:35,909
One carried AVIRIS-NG, reading sediment
in the water and the plants below.

31
00:01:35,932 --> 00:01:37,511
And a third carried UAVSAR,

32
00:01:37,546 --> 00:01:40,657
a radar that tracks how water
levels change inside the marsh.

33
00:01:40,692 --> 00:01:43,037
Together, they map where sediment ends up,

34
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:46,149
and which parts of the delta
can keep up with the rising sea.

35
00:01:46,323 --> 00:01:49,852
The lead researcher said the gains
show that, with enough restoration

36
00:01:49,899 --> 00:01:53,788
to bring back fresh water and sediment,
some of these wetlands could recover.

37
00:01:54,113 --> 00:01:55,994
And it matters far beyond Louisiana.

38
00:01:56,040 --> 00:01:58,502
About three hundred fifty
million people live and farm

39
00:01:58,571 --> 00:02:00,232
on river deltas around the world.

40
00:02:00,475 --> 00:02:03,854
For another place where the map
is changing, watch this one next.
